"The prognosis is driven by several significant factors, including a high preoperative PSA of 86 and positive surgical margins. Pathological findings are concerning due to the presence of extraprostatic extension and seminal vesicle invasion. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison shows a discrepancy, with the biopsy identifying Gleason 4+4 while the prostatectomy specimen was Gleason 4+3 (ISUP grade group 3). Node metastasis was documented, which contributes to the elevated risk score.\n\nCountervailing findings include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and that no seminal vesicle invasion details were provided beyond simple documentation of invasion. The current data lacks postoperative PSA measurements or follow-up information, which would be crucial for refining prognosis. Future trajectory assessment is highly dependent on subsequent PSA levels and their pattern over time. The fixed estimated horizon is 31.18 months. This is a predicted recurrence horizon, not evidence of observed recurrence. The sensitivity range is 24.94–37.42 months; its coverage has not been validated."
